WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.680
The triplet loss is one good way to learn the parameters of a conf net for phase recognition.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.520
There's another way to learn these parameters.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:14.080
Let me show you how phase recognition can also be posed as a straight binary classification

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.080
problem.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.440
Another way to train a neural network is to take this pair of neural networks, to

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:26.360
take this Siamese network, and have them both compute these embeddings, maybe 128

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.060
dimensional embeddings, maybe even higher dimensional.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:36.680
And then have these be input to a logistic regression unit to then just make a prediction

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:43.880
where the target output will be 1 if both of these are the same persons and 0 if both

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.640
of these are different persons.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:53.380
So this is a way to treat phase recognition just as a binary classification problem.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:58.980
And this is an alternative to the triplet loss for training a system like this.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.500
Now what does this final logistic regression unit actually do?

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:10.980
The output y hat will be, let's say a sigmoid function applied to some set of features.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:17.860
But rather than just feeding in these encodings, what you can do is take the differences between

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:18.860
the encodings.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.020
So let me show you what I mean.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:30.600
Let's say I write a sum over k equals 1 to 128 of let's say the absolute value taken

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.860
element wise between the two different encodings.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:40.060
So let me just finish writing this out and then we'll see what this means.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:46.100
So in this notation, f of xi is the encoding of the image xi.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:52.460
And this subscript k means to just select out the kth component of this vector.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:59.880
So this is taking the element wise difference in absolute values between these two encodings.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:05.700
And what you might do is think of these 128 numbers as features that you then feed into

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.540
logistic regression.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.980
And your final logistic regression can have additional parameters wi and b, similar to

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.300
a normal logistic regression unit.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:23.100
And you would train an appropriate weighting on these 128 features in order to predict

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:28.460
whether or not these two images are of the same person or of different persons.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:34.500
So this would be one pretty reasonable way to learn to predict 0 or 1, whether these

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.660
are the same person or different persons.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.900
And there are a few other variations on how you can compute this formula that I had

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.540
underlined in green.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:55.020
For example, another formula could be this, k minus f of xj, k squared divided by f of

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:00.580
xi plus f of xj, k.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.100
This is sometimes called the chi-squared formula.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.940
This is the Greek alphabet chi.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:09.180
And this is sometimes called the chi-squared similarity.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.140
And this and other variations are explored.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:16.700
And this and other variations are explored in this deep-face paper, which I had referenced

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.700
earlier as well.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:23.820
But so in this learning formulation, the input is a pair of images.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:31.020
So this is really your training input x, and the output y is either 0 or 1, depending on

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:36.020
whether you're inputting a pair of similar or dissimilar images.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:39.100
And same as before, you're training a Siamese network.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.980
So that means that this neural network up here has parameters that are the same, or they're

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.020
really tied to the parameters in this lower neural network.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.740
And this system can work pretty well as well.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:56.900
Lastly, I just want to mention one computational trick that can help you deployment

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.780
significantly, which is that if this is the new image, so this is an employee walking

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:07.020
in hoping that the turnstile, the doorway, will open for them, and if this is from your

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:14.100
database image, then instead of having to compute this set of features, then instead

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:18.860
of having to compute this embedding every single time, what you can do is actually

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.020
pre-compute that.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:27.180
So when the new employee walks in, what you can do is use this upper conf net to compute

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:34.020
that encoding, and use it to then compare it to your pre-computed encoding, and then use

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.960
that to make a prediction y hat.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:43.140
So because you don't need to store the raw images, and also because if you have a very

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:48.980
large database of employees, you don't need to compute these encodings every single time

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:54.180
for every employee in your database, this idea of pre-computing some of these encodings

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:57.180
can save significant computation.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.740
And this type of pre-computation works for this type of Siamese network architecture, where

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:09.620
you treat face recognition as a binary classification problem, as well as for when you were learning

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:15.260
encodings, maybe using the triplet loss function as described in the last couple of videos.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:20.940
And so just to wrap up, to treat face verification as supervised learning, you create a training

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:26.340
set of just pairs of images now, instead of triplets, pairs of images, where the target

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:33.020
label is 1, when these are a pair of pictures of the same person, and where the target

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:40.980
label is 0, when these are pictures of different persons, and you use different pairs to train

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.820
the neural network to train the Siamese network using backpropagation.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:51.860
So this version that you just saw of treating face verification and by extension face recognition

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.820
as a binary classification problem, this works quite well as well.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.220
And so with that, I hope that you now know what it would take to train your own face

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:04.980
verification or your own face recognition system, one that can do one-shot learning.
